            <ArticleTitle>Intracranial displacement of the eye after blunt trauma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>311</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/WNO.0b013e3181bef65b</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 67-year-old man fell from an agricultural vehicle and struck his right eye on a protruding element. Eight hours later, he was brought to the emergency unit of an ophthalmology hospital where examiners could not find the right eye and believed it to have been completely destroyed. However, CT disclosed that the eye, apparently still intact, had been displaced into the anterior cranial fossa through a fracture in the orbital roof. This is the first documentation of such a phenomenon.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
